{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"60186d2b-390c-451a-bbb5-299cfef9af20","step_index":18,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":0.645167,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"grep -A 15 \"## Worked examples\" 01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/*.md"},"output":"\u003ctruncated 54 lines\u003e\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-Consider an application performing updates on a 128-byte data structure.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-An application issues 5 consecutive \"no-flush\" transactions that each call `set_range` on the exact same 128-byte block.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-Without inter-transaction optimization, RVM would buffer 5 separate redo records.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-Each record consists of an 8-byte transaction header, an 8-byte range header, 128 bytes of payload, and 8 bytes of bidirectional displacements.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-The unoptimized log size would be 5 * (8 + 8 + 128 + 8) = 760 bytes.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-However, with inter-transaction optimization, RVM recognizes that all 5 transactions modify the same memory.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-It coalesces them, keeping only the payload from the final transaction.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-The final flushed log size is just 1 * (8 + 8 + 128 + 8) = 152 bytes.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-This optimization reduces the required disk I/O for this block by exactly 80 percent.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-## Comparison\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08a-Lightweight-Recoverable-Virtual-Memory.md-\r\n--\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md:## Worked examples\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-Standard LRVM on a raw disk incurs an overhead of 10,000 microseconds for a small transaction, dominated by the synchronous disk I/O to the redo log.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-LRVM running on Rio (RVM-Rio) short-circuits the disk I/O, dropping the overhead to 500 microseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-This remaining 500 microseconds is due to system calls like `write` and `fsync`, and copying to the redo log.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-Vista on Rio eliminates the redo log, system calls, and multiple copies, dropping the overhead down to 5 microseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-The speedup over RVM-Rio is 500 / 5 = 100x.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-The speedup over standard RVM is 10000 / 5 = 2000x.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-For Vista transactions larger than 1 KB, the overhead scales linearly at a rate of 17.9 microseconds per KB.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-If a transaction modifies an 8 KB chunk of memory, the overhead is the base 5 microseconds plus the 7 KB over the baseline times 17.9 microseconds per KB.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-The calculated overhead is 5 + (7 * 17.9) = 5 + 125.3 = 130.3 microseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-## Comparison\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08b-RioVista.md-| Feature | LRVM | LRVM on Rio | Vista on Rio |\r\n--\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md:## Worked examples\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-Consider the transaction latency in Quicksilver for a distributed commit involving three nodes.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-Assume local interprocess communication takes 2 milliseconds, network message delay is 10 milliseconds, and a synchronous log force takes 15 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-When Node A initiates a commit, it sends a vote request over the network to Node B, taking 10 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-Node B receives the request and sends a vote request to its subordinate Node C, taking another 10 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-Node C processes the vote, forces its log which takes 15 milliseconds, and replies to Node B taking 10 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-Node B receives the vote, forces its log taking 15 milliseconds, and replies to Node A taking 10 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-Node A receives the final vote and forces the global commit log taking 15 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-The total time for the voting phase to complete before Node A decides to commit is 10 plus 10 plus 15 plus 10 plus 15 plus 10 plus 15, equating to 85 milliseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-This calculation demonstrates the critical path of the hierarchical commit protocol and the substantial impact of synchronous disk operations.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-## Comparison\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-| Feature | Quicksilver | TxOS | Percolator |\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L08c-Quicksilver.md-| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n"}}}
