{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"60186d2b-390c-451a-bbb5-299cfef9af20","step_index":20,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":0.157478,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"grep -A 15 \"## Worked examples\" 01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07*.md"},"output":"\u003ctruncated 1 lines\u003e\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-**Epoch arithmetic.**\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Use the toy in the initiator section.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-M = 4, weights 0, 1, and 3.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-The forward probability to C is 3/4 = 0.75.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Suppose over the epoch the cluster forwards 80 pages that were younger than MinAge, and discards 40 that were older.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Expected forwards to C are 0.75 x 80 = 60.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Expected forwards to B are 0.25 x 80 = 20.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Expected forwards to A are 0.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-C, holding the largest weight, is allowed to end the epoch after it has accepted w_C = 3 pages in the toy rule.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Scale the same fractions to the paper's \"thousands of replacements\": if M = 1000 and w_C = 600, C expects 60 percent of the forwards and ends the epoch after 600 arrivals.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-**Hit versus disk.**\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-Non-shared getpage hit total is 61 + 156 + 8 + 1135 + 80 = 1440 microseconds.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07a-Global-Memory-Systems.md-The components are request generation, reply receipt, GCD processing, network hardware and software, and target PFD processing.\r\n--\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md:## Worked examples\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-**Diff Size and Garbage Collection Overhead**\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-Imagine a DSM system with a 4096-byte page size.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-1. Node A modifies a single 4-byte integer on the page.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-2. At lock release, Node A compares the modified page to the 4096-byte twin.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-3. The diff identifies only the 4-byte change.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-With run-length encoding (RLE) overhead (an 8-byte header specifying the offset and length), the diff size is just 12 bytes.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-4. Sending 12 bytes over the network is vastly cheaper than sending a full 4096-byte page.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-**GC Trigger Math:**\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-If the system generates an average diff size of 32 bytes per lock release, and the garbage collection space threshold is set to 1MB of pending diffs:\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-$1,048,576 \\text{ bytes} / 32 \\text{ bytes/diff} = 32,768 \\text{ diffs}$\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-The system can endure roughly 32,768 critical sections before the space metric forces a garbage collection cycle to compact the diffs into the master page and invalidate caches.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07b-Distributed-Shared-Memory.md-## Comparison\r\n--\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md:## Worked examples\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-Let us examine the parity overhead and block distribution in an xFS stripe group.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-Suppose we have a stripe group of 5 storage servers ($N=5$) utilizing a single parity disk.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-The stripe segment size is 1024 KB.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-1. The 1024 KB segment is divided into $N-1 = 4$ data fragments.\r\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-2. Each data fragment size is $1024 / 4 = 256$ KB.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-3. The client computes a parity fragment of 256 KB.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-4. The client writes 256 KB to Server 1, 256 KB to Server 2, 256 KB to Server 3, 256 KB to Server 4, and the 256 KB parity to Server 5.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-5. The total data transferred over the network is $1024 + 256 = 1280$ KB.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-6. The parity overhead is $256 / 1024 = 0.25$, or 25 percent.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-If Server 3 fails, the system reconstructs its 256 KB fragment by XORing the 256 KB fragments from Server 1, Server 2, Server 4, and the Parity from Server 5.\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-\r\n01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-4-Distributed-Subsystems-and-Recovery/L07c-Distributed-File-Systems.md-## Comparison\r\n"}}}
